{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThree unit tests sleep through real production backoff, so CI pays wall-clock for\ndelays that are pure logic. Confirmed against current main in this worktree:\n\n* `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/access_check.py:44` declares\n  `sleep_fn=time.sleep` — an IMPORT-BOUND default, evaluated once at def time.\n  `tests/scripts/bridge/test_jira_capability_probe.py:286` does\n  `mock.patch(\"time.sleep\")  # avoid real delay`, which rebinds the attribute on\n  the `time` module and therefore cannot reach the already-captured default. The\n  probe's JQL retry then really sleeps `JQL_RETRY_SLEEP=5` five times\n  (`JQL_RETRY_COUNT=6`, `access_check.py:12-13,104-112`) = 25s, matching the\n  filed 25.3s.\n* `scripts/dependency_audit.py:194` has the same shape,\n  `sleeper: Callable[[float], None] = time.sleep`, and\n  `tests/scripts/test_dependency_audit.py:319` patches `mod.time.sleep` — again\n  too late. `run_pip_audit` sleeps `attempt * 5` for attempts 1 and 2\n  (`dependency_audit.py:215-216`) = 5s + 10s = 15s, matching the filed 15.0s.\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_applier_stale_binding_404.py:236`\n  (`test_non_404_http_error_still_propagates`) patches nothing at all. It drives\n  `_call_with_retry` (`src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py:74`),\n  whose HTTPError-5xx branch sleeps `2**(attempt+1) + random.random()`\n  (line 129) for three retries = ~2+4+8 plus jitter ≈ 14-17s, matching the\n  filed 15.8s.\n\nSo the premise holds for all three, and the two \"patched\" tests were silently\nno-ops — the comment `# avoid real delay` was already false.\n\n## Approach\n\nLate-bind the two import-bound defaults, and make all three tests use a RECORDING\nsleeper that asserts the logical schedule.\n\n* In `access_check.run_access_check` and `dependency_audit.run_pip_audit`, change\n  the default from `time.sleep` to `None` and resolve\n  `sleep_fn = time.sleep if sleep_fn is None else sleep_fn` in the body. Production\n  behaviour is byte-identical: both parameters are keyword-only, no caller in the\n  tree passes them (`_lib_ops.py:303` and `jira-capability-probe.py:34` are the only\n  `run_access_check` call sites, neither passes `sleep_fn`), and an unpassed argument\n  still resolves to `time.sleep` — only WHEN it resolves moves, from import time to\n  call time, which is what makes the seam patchable at all.\n* Each of the three tests replaces its ineffective (or absent) patch with a sleeper\n  that appends the requested delay to a list and returns immediately, then asserts\n  the recorded schedule: `[5, 5, 5, 5, 5]` for the probe, `[5, 10]` for the audit,\n  and three HTTPError backoffs for the 500 test. `dispatch_apply_phases.py:132`\n  calls a bare `time.sleep(delay)` resolved at call time, so the 500 test needs only\n  a `monkeypatch.setattr(time, \"sleep\", recorder)` — no production change there.\n\nAsserting the recorded schedule IS the required guard: if a future change re-binds\nthe seam early, or a new call path bypasses it, the recorder stays empty and the\nassertion fails loudly, rather than the test merely getting slow again. That is a\nstronger guard than checking wall time, and it adds no timing-budget assertion.\n\nLate-binding is preferred over threading an explicit sleeper down from `main`,\nbecause the probe is executed as a script through `run_access_check()` with no\ninjection point, so a caller-supplied sleeper could not reach it without changing\nthe probe's public entry shape.\n\n## Scope\n\n* `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/access_check.py` — late-bind `sleep_fn`.\n* `scripts/dependency_audit.py` — late-bind `sleeper`.\n* `tests/scripts/bridge/test_jira_capability_probe.py` — recording sleeper +\n  schedule assertion in `test_probe_retries_jql_search_three_times`.\n* `tests/scripts/test_dependency_audit.py` — recording sleeper + schedule\n  assertion in `test_gate_reports_db_unreachable_as_infrastructure`.\n* `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/apply/test_applier_stale_binding_404.py` —\n  recording sleeper + schedule assertion in\n  `test_non_404_http_error_still_propagates`.\n\n`dispatch_apply_phases.py` is read but NOT modified; its bare `time.sleep` is\nalready a working seam.\n\n## Testing\n\n* Happy path — each of the three tests still asserts exactly what it asserted\n  before (6 `search_issues` calls; 3 `pip-audit` attempts with the INFRASTRUCTURE\n  message; `pytest.raises(urllib.error.HTTPError)` on the 500), and now\n  additionally asserts its logical delay schedule.\n* Failure/edge path — the guard is proven by seeding: reverting\n  `access_check.py` to the import-bound default is confirmed to make the probe\n  test FAIL on the empty recorder (not merely run slowly), and the same for\n  `dependency_audit.py`. Both seeds reverted; neither committed.\n* Production-behaviour check — `run_access_check` and `run_pip_audit` called\n  without a sleeper still resolve to `time.sleep`, confirmed by a direct\n  interpreter check of the resolved default rather than by sleeping.\n* Whole-suite regression — the CI pytest command is green.\n\nNo wall-clock or timing-budget assertion is added; savings go in ticket comments.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] `test_probe_retries_jql_search_three_times` still asserts\n      `search_issues.call_count == 6` AND asserts the recorded delays are five\n      5-second entries, with no real sleep — verified by the test passing in\n      under 2s where it took ~25s.\n- [x] `test_gate_reports_db_unreachable_as_infrastructure` still asserts rc 1,\n      `INFRASTRUCTURE issue`, `recheck`, and three `pip-audit` attempts, AND\n      asserts recorded delays `[5, 10]` — verified by the test passing in under\n      2s where it took ~15s.\n- [x] `test_non_404_http_error_still_propagates` still raises\n      `urllib.error.HTTPError` after retry exhaustion AND asserts three recorded\n      backoff delays — verified by the test passing in under 2s where it took\n      ~15s.\n- [x] Production retry counts, delay schedules, exception types and messages are\n      unchanged: the only production edits are the two default values moving from\n      `time.sleep` to `None` with an equivalent in-body resolution, shown by\n      `git diff` on the two production files being exactly that.\n- [x] Each of the three tests fails (not merely slows) when its seam is reverted\n      to the import-bound default — recorded as a ticket comment with the seeded\n      failure output, seeds reverted.\n- [x] Before/after wall-clock for the three tests is recorded as a ticket comment,\n      measured with `pytest --durations`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786607499374875001,"uuid":"919128a9-5d10-4441-9875-deaec30a8d11"}